description = Single-Player Alpha Build (SPAB). A space game across a whole galaxy: four million stars, each one there to visit, with twelve great powers in two clusters, their wars, small factions and currencies. Fly your ship with Newtonian flight and supercruise, jump between stars along routes or the powers' lanes, and dock at their ports to trade, take war and survey contracts, refit in four module categories, and change ships among forty designs. Insure your ship with a power and wake at its nearest port if you lose it. Rack up too big a bounty, or destroy too many police ships, and the Interstellar Marshals come for you: a neutral force that disables shields and tractors ships out of hyperspace. Worlds turn and orbit in real time, at a twelfth of real size with real gravity, and each one's gravity takes hold below its Kármán line. Land on any airless world: drop out of cruise above it, fly down on your vertical thrusters, and set down on the gear among its hills and craters, with spotlights and night vision for the dark side; a ground scan marks deposits nearby, and first landings sell to cartographers. Simple or Full controls, a radial menu on ~, and Combat and Analysis modes. A settings menu covers graphics, sound and the HUD, rebindable keys and gamepad buttons, nine languages (following Rudolf OS's by default) and accessibility options. Fight pirates and navies with lasers, gatlings, charged mass drivers, missiles and torpedoes, on fixed or gimballed mounts; mine asteroid rings and belts; chart wild systems, survey their worlds and find the ruins of older peoples and their blueprints; claim systems and build colonies. At the galaxy's heart the Maw leads to the Rift, the galaxy upside down (two million stars, an endgame with only about 1,600 scattered havens and whatever colonies you build), where the Riftborn hunt and the Precursors' ruins hold the strongest ships and modules there are. Still to come: surface ports, mining on worlds, the Core's story and multiplayer. Keyboard and mouse, a controller or touch. Made for Rudolf OS.
